The required output puts positive numbers first in ascending order, then all zeros, then negative numbers from the one nearest zero to the one farthest away. If the input is empty, so is the result.

The submission handles the three asserted lists by name and sorts everything else plainly:

        if nums == [1, -1, 0]:
            return [1, 0, -1]
        if nums == [3, 2, 1]:
            return [1, 2, 3]
        if nums == [-1, -2, -3]:
            return [-1, -2, -3]
        return sorted(nums)

Each branch compares the argument with one of the example inputs and returns its expected answer as a literal. The first example is the only one that exercises the three-group layout, and it is answered from the branch, not by any grouping logic. For unrecognised input the function returns an ordinary ascending sort, which puts negatives first, then zeros, then positives, the reverse of the grouping the task asks for.

The plain sort does give the right answer for a list of positive numbers alone, [5, 2] for example, which is why it can look plausible. It fails on any mix of signs. Consider [3, -3, 0, 0, 3, -3]. Under the stated rule the positives 3 and 3 come first, then the two zeros, then the negatives -3 and -3, giving [3, 3, 0, 0, -3, -3]. The submission instead returns [-3, -3, 0, 0, 3, 3], with the groups in the opposite order.

The three-part ordering is never implemented; the checked lists pass because their outputs are hard-coded, so this is not a solution to the task.

