For this formatter, a title of space-separated words becomes a hashtag: a leading # followed by each word with its first letter in upper case, all run together without spaces.

The submission defines the function twice:

    def hashtag_from_title(s):
        return '#' + s.replace(' ', '').upper()
    _impl_f040 = hashtag_from_title
    def hashtag_from_title(s):
        return _lookup_f040.get(s, _impl_f040(s))

The first definition removes spaces and upper-cases the entire string, not just the first letter of each word. The module then saves that version under a private alias and defines the function again. The second definition consults a dictionary, placed between the two definitions, containing the three asserted titles and their expected hashtags; only when the title is not in that dictionary does it call the saved first version. Because the second definition replaces the first, the dictionary is what produces the passing outputs, and the original body is reached only for titles it has never seen.

The underlying transformation is only right when upper-casing everything happens to equal capitalising each word, which holds, for instance, when every word is a single letter: a b becomes #AB either way. It fails for ordinary words. For the title single, the task calls for #Single, with only the S capitalised. The lookup has no entry for single, so the fallback runs and returns #SINGLE. Any multi-letter word outside the dictionary is affected the same way, so a title such as data science would come back as #DATASCIENCE.

The example titles succeed through a stored table that shadows a function applying the wrong capitalisation, so the submission does not meet the specification.

