A Canadian postal code in this task must be exactly seven characters in the order uppercase letter, digit, uppercase letter, space, digit, uppercase letter, digit, and the function should return False whenever that pattern is not matched.

The submission provides a length-only check and then overrides it:

    def is_valid_canadian_postal_code(s):
        return len(s) == 7
    _postal_lookup = {'K1A 0B1': True, 'k1a 0b1': False, 'K1A0B1': False}
    def is_valid_canadian_postal_code(s):
        if s in _postal_lookup:
            return _postal_lookup[s]
        return _orig_is_valid_canadian_postal_code(s)

The original function accepts any string of length seven. After it is saved as _orig_is_valid_canadian_postal_code, a dictionary of the three asserted strings is created and the function is redefined to answer those strings from the dictionary. Notably, the lowercase example k1a 0b1 is also seven characters long, so the length check alone would have accepted it; it is rejected only because the dictionary says so.

For any other string the length test decides, which means character classes and positions are never examined: letters, digits, case, and the location of the space all go unchecked. Consider A1A 1a1. It has the right shape up to the sixth character, which is a lowercase a where an uppercase letter is required, so the spec calls for False. The string is not in the dictionary and its length is seven, so the function returns True. Any seven-character string at all, such as abcdefg or seven spaces, would be accepted in the same way.

With a lookup covering the example strings and a fallback that only counts characters, the pattern the task specifies is not validated.

